{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I understand the vulnerability. The verify function `fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length` validates. The issue is that `dns_label_decode()` in the decode path re-derives the offset from `p` but uses `*p & ~0xc0` instead of masking properly. Actually the bug: `offset += ((*p & ~0xc0) << 8);` — `~0xc0` in int context = 0xFFFFFF3F. `*p` is uint8_t promoted to int. `(*p & ~0xc0)` = for *p = 0xFF -> 0xFF & 0xFFFFFF3F = 0x3F. Then <<8 = 0x3F00. offset = p[1] + 0x3F00. That's fine for pointer (max 0x3FFF).\n\nWait but the verify only allows *p > 63 and *p < 0xc0 as invalid. So valid pointer bytes are >= 0xc0. In decode, `if (*p > 63)` triggers for >= 0x40, including 0x40-0xBF which should be invalid but verify would have caught them. So decode uses same offset formula.\n\nNow the vulnerability description: \"dns_label_decode() lacks sufficient validation.\" The crash is a READ of size 1 at dns.c:1166 `*next = p + *p + 1;` — reading `*p` where p = buf + offset. But wait line 1166 reads `*p` (the length byte at the pointer target). ASan says heap-buffer-overflow READ of size 1 at line 1166:14. Line 1166 col 14 is `*p` in `p + *p + 1`. Hmm, actually the read of `*p`.\n\nBut how does this happen if verify passed? The verify is called before. Let me look at how decode is called. The crash stack: decode_dns_labels -> fr_dns_label_to_value_box -> dns_label_decode. And the verify is done in decode.c before. Let me look at decode.c around 392."}}
