rify. For the array case, it calls `fr_dns_labels_network_verify`.\n\nThe `dns_label_decode` is called in `fr_dns_label_to_value_box`, which is called for each label in the loop `for (total = 0; total < labels_len; total += slen)`.\n\nNow, `fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length` computes `after` = the end of the label, and `slen` = the uncompressed length. Then `fr_dns_label_to_value_box` re-walks from `label` to `after`, calling `dns_label_decode`.\n\nThe key issue: `dns_label_decode` recomputes the label boundaries WITHOUT the validation that `fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length` had. So if the label contains a compression pointer, `dns_label_decode` follows it but does NOT validate that the target is within bounds or points to a valid label. Wait, but `fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length` DID validate it when computing length. Unless the pointer is the FIRST thing and... \n\nHmm, let me look again at the `*next = p + *p + 1` in dns_label_decode. When p is a compression pointer target, `*p` is the length byte. So next = target + length + 1. That's the byte after the pointed-to label.\n\nBut here's the thing: in `fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length`, after following the pointer, it validates `(p + *p + 1) > end` for the pointed-to label (end = buf + buf_len). Then p += *p+1.\n\nThe `after` value = p (where p stopped, i.e., after the pointer bytes at the original position, since `*next = p+2` when jumping). Wait, when following a pointer, `*next = p + 2` where p is the position of the pointer. So `after` = the byte after the 2-byte pointer in the ORIGINAL location.\n\nThen in `fr_dns_label_to_value_box`, `after` is that. `slen` (uncompressed length) includes all labels.\n\nThe while loop: `current = label` (original position). `dns_label_decode(packet, &current, &next)`. current points at the pointer byte (e.g., 0xC0). `*p > 63` so it's a pointer: offset computed, p = buf + offset. Then `*start = p` (current = pointed-to location), `*next = p + *p + 1` (byte after pointed-to labe